  "query_preview": "..Broad high pressure centered east of the Four\nCorners area along with a weak shortwave trough lifting across \nthe PacNW has brought a drier southwesterly flow to Utah. This has\nreduced PWATs to around 0.9 inches in southern and central Utah, \nalthough a pocket of 1-1.2 inches exists across northwest Utah. \nConvection across southern Utah remained fairly isolated across \nsouthern Utah today as a result. Across northwest Utah, however, \nthe moisture pocket and associated instability combined with \nincreased shear owing to the proximity of the upper jet has \nallowed more scattered convection along with stronger storms \ntoday. These storms will continue to cross north-central Utah \nthrough late this evening before dissipating. An outflow boundary\nassociated with these storms will continue to bring enhanced \nwinds to portions of northern Utah until shortly after midnight.\n\nThe drier southwesterly flow will continue through Sunday and into\nearly Monday. PWATs below 1 inch will spread into northern Utah\nSunday. While this will bring a decreased threat of convection to\nnorthern Utah, a vort max rotating around the high is progged to\nlift across southeast Utah. This will aid in bringing increased\nconvection to portions of southern and central Utah once again.\n\nDespite the drying, enough moisture will be around to keep low\ntemperatures elevated. In the valleys, this means that many valley \nlocations will stay in the 70s to around 80 overnight with high\ntemperatures rising above 100F, with the lack of relief\ncontributing to an increased risk for heat related illness. As a\nresult, heat highlights remain in place through Monday. \n\nAs the ridge amplifies, expect the flow aloft to turn more\nsoutherly, drawing moisture into the area once again late Monday\nor Tuesday. As such, a period of more active convection is\nexpected to return, but this should be relatively brief. The high\npressure will start to drift westward mid/late week, ending up\nnear southwest UT/northwest AZ for the weekend. This will bring a\ndrier airmass into the area, with the drying trend beginning as \nearly as Thursday, and could potentially continue through at least\nthe early part of next week.",
